1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:05,920
The term “cyberspace” was coined by William Gibson, a science-fiction writer.
“网络空间”一词是由科幻作家威廉·吉布森创造的。

2
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:20,050
He first used it in a short story in 1982, and expanded on it a couple of years later in a novel, “Neuromancer”, whose main character, Henry Dorsett Case, is a troubled computer hacker and drug addict.
他于1982年在一篇短篇小说中首次使用这个词，两年后又在长篇小说《神经漫游者》中对它作了进一步阐述；这部小说的主人公亨利·多塞特·凯斯是一个麻烦缠身的电脑黑客兼瘾君子。

3
00:00:20,850 --> 00:00:34,860
In the book Mr. Gibson describes cyberspace as “a consensual hallucination experienced daily by billions of legitimate operators” and “a graphic representation of data abstracted from the banks of every computer in the human system.”
吉布森在书中把网络空间描述为“数十亿合法操作者每天都在体验的一种共识幻觉”和“数据的图形化表示，这些数据抽取自人类系统中每一台电脑的存储库”。

4
00:00:35,660 --> 00:00:39,220
His literary creation turned out to be remarkably prescient.
事实证明，他的这一文学创造有着惊人的先见之明。

5
00:00:40,020 --> 00:00:51,140
Cyberspace has become shorthand for the computing devices, networks, fibre-optic cables, wireless links and other infrastructure that bring the internet to billions of people around the world.
网络空间已成为一个简称，泛指把互联网带给全世界数十亿人的计算设备、网络、光纤电缆、无线链路及其他基础设施。

6
00:00:51,940 --> 00:01:02,150
The myriad connections forged by these technologies have brought tremendous benefits to everyone who uses the web to tap into humanity’s collective store of knowledge every day.
这些技术建立起的无数连接，给每天借助网络从人类集体知识宝库中汲取知识的所有人都带来了巨大的好处。

7
00:01:02,950 --> 00:01:06,140
But there is a darker side to this extraordinary invention.
但这项非凡的发明也有其阴暗的一面。

8
00:01:06,940 --> 00:01:10,210
Data breaches are becoming ever bigger and more common.
数据泄露事件规模越来越大，也越来越常见。

9
00:01:11,010 --> 00:01:15,930
Last year over 800m records were lost, mainly through such attacks.
去年有超过8亿条记录丢失，主要是由于这类攻击。

10
00:01:16,730 --> 00:01:36,240
Among the most prominent recent victims has been Target, whose chief executive, Gregg Steinhafel, stood down from his job in May, a few months after the giant American retailer revealed that online intruders had stolen millions of digital records about its customers, including credit- and debit-card details.
近来最引人注目的受害者之一是塔吉特公司：这家美国零售业巨头披露，网络入侵者窃取了数以百万计的客户数字记录，其中包括信用卡和借记卡的详细信息；几个月后，该公司首席执行官格雷格·斯坦哈菲尔于5月辞去了职务。

11
00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:44,130
Other well-known firms such as Adobe, a tech company, and eBay, an online marketplace, have also been hit.
其他知名公司，比如科技公司Adobe和网上交易平台eBay，也遭到了攻击。

12
00:01:44,930 --> 00:01:49,500
The potential damage, though, extends well beyond such commercial incursions.
不过，潜在的危害远远超出了这类商业入侵的范围。

13
00:01:50,300 --> 00:02:11,250
Wider concerns have been raised by the revelations made by Edward Snowden, a contractor to America’s National Security Agency (NSA), about the mass surveillance carried out by Western intelligence agencies, as well as by the growing numbers of cyber-warriors being recruited by countries that see cyberspace as a new domain of warfare.
美国国家安全局（NSA）的合同工爱德华·斯诺登披露了西方情报机构实施的大规模监控，而那些把网络空间视为新作战领域的国家正在招募越来越多的网络战士，这两方面都引发了更广泛的担忧。

14
00:02:12,050 --> 00:02:23,540
America’s president, Barack Obama, said in a White House press release earlier this year that cyberthreats “pose one of the gravest national-security dangers” the country is facing.
美国总统巴拉克·奥巴马今年早些时候在一份白宫新闻稿中称，网络威胁“构成了”美国当前面临的“最严重的国家安全危险之一”。

15
00:02:24,340 --> 00:02:32,110
Securing cyberspace is hard because the architecture of the internet was designed to promote connectivity, not security.
保障网络空间的安全很难，因为互联网的架构是为促进互联互通而设计的，并不是为了安全。

16
00:02:32,910 --> 00:02:40,780
Its founders focused on getting it to work and did not worry much about threats because the network was affiliated with America’s military.
互联网的创建者们专注于让它运转起来，并不怎么担心威胁，因为这个网络隶属于美国军方。

17
00:02:41,580 --> 00:02:49,510
As hackers turned up, layers of security, from antivirus programs to firewalls, were added to try to keep them at bay.
随着黑客陆续出现，人们加上了一层又一层安全防护，从杀毒程序到防火墙，试图把黑客挡在门外。

18
00:02:50,310 --> 00:02:58,940
Gartner, a research firm, reckons that last year organisations around the globe spent $67 billion on information security.
研究公司高德纳估计，去年全球各机构在信息安全上花了670亿美元。

19
00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:03,280
On the whole, these defences have worked reasonably well.
总的来说，这些防御措施的效果一直还算不错。

20
00:03:04,080 --> 00:03:11,280
For all the talk about the risk of a “cyber 9/11” or a “cybergeddon”, the internet has proved remarkably resilient.
尽管人们大谈发生“网络9·11”或“网络末日”的风险，事实证明，互联网的韧性出奇地强。

21
00:03:12,080 --> 00:03:26,020
Hundreds of millions of people turn on their computers every day and bank online, shop at virtual stores, swap gossip and photos with their friends on social networks and send all kinds of sensitive data over the web without ill effect.
每天都有数以亿计的人打开电脑，在网上办理银行业务、在虚拟商店购物、在社交网络上与朋友交换八卦和照片，并通过网络发送各种敏感数据，都没有受到不良影响。

22
00:03:26,820 --> 00:03:30,700
Companies and governments are shifting ever more services online.
企业和政府正把越来越多的服务转移到网上。

23
00:03:31,500 --> 00:03:33,340
But the task is becoming harder.
但保障网络空间安全这项任务正变得越来越难。

24
00:03:34,140 --> 00:03:45,650
Cyber-security, which involves protecting both data and people, is facing multiple threats, notably cybercrime and online industrial espionage, both of which are growing rapidly.
网络安全既涉及保护数据，也涉及保护人，目前正面临多重威胁，尤其是网络犯罪和网上工业间谍活动，两者都在迅速增长。

25
00:03:46,450 --> 00:04:06,090
A recent estimate by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a think-tank, puts the annual global cost of digital crime and intellectual-property theft at $445 billion—a sum roughly equivalent to the GDP of a smallish rich European country such as Austria.
智库战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）最近估计，数字犯罪和知识产权盗窃每年给全球造成的损失为4450亿美元，这一数额大致相当于奥地利这类规模偏小的欧洲富国的国内生产总值。

26
00:04:06,890 --> 00:04:10,780
To add to the worries, there is also the risk of cyber-sabotage.
更令人担忧的是，还存在网络破坏的风险。

27
00:04:11,580 --> 00:04:23,800
Terrorists or agents of hostile powers could mount attacks on companies and systems that control vital parts of an economy, including power stations, electrical grids and communications networks.
恐怖分子或敌对国家的特工可能会对一些企业和系统发动攻击，这些企业和系统控制着一国经济的关键部分，包括发电站、电网和通信网络。

28
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:27,930
Such attacks are hard to pull off, but not impossible.
这类攻击很难得手，但并非不可能。

29
00:04:28,730 --> 00:04:40,600
One precedent is the destruction in 2010 of centrifuges at a nuclear facility in Iran by a computer program known as Stuxnet, the handiwork of American and Israeli software experts.
一个先例是：2010年，一款名为“震网”的计算机程序摧毁了伊朗一座核设施里的离心机，这款程序出自美国和以色列软件专家之手。

30
00:04:41,400 --> 00:04:42,860
But such events are rare.
但这类事件很少见。

31
00:04:43,660 --> 00:04:57,300
The biggest day-to-day threats faced by companies and government agencies come from crooks and spooks hoping to steal financial data and trade secrets, so this special report will focus mainly on cybercrime and cyber-espionage.
企业和政府机构日常面临的最大威胁，来自企图窃取财务数据和商业机密的犯罪分子和间谍，因此本特别报道将主要关注网络犯罪和网络间谍活动。

32
00:04:58,100 --> 00:05:08,660
Smarter, better-organised hackers are making life tougher for the cyber-defenders, but the report will argue that even so a number of things can be done to keep everyone safer than they are now.
更聪明、组织更严密的黑客正让网络防御者的日子越来越难过，但本报道将论证，即便如此，仍有一些办法可以让每个人都比现在更安全。

33
00:05:09,460 --> 00:05:13,960
One is to ensure that organisations get the basics of cyber-security right.
其中一个办法是确保各机构把网络安全的基本功做好。

34
00:05:14,760 --> 00:05:24,390
All too often breaches are caused by simple blunders, such as failing to separate systems containing sensitive data from those that do not need access to them.
安全防线屡屡因简单的失误而被攻破，比如没有把存有敏感数据的系统与无需访问这些数据的系统隔开。

35
00:05:25,190 --> 00:05:33,990
Companies also need to get better at anticipating where attacks may be coming from and at adapting their defences swiftly in response to new threats.
企业还需要更善于预判攻击可能来自何方，也要更善于针对新威胁迅速调整防御措施。

36
00:05:34,790 --> 00:05:41,770
Technology can help, as can industry initiatives that allow firms to share intelligence about risks with each other.
技术能帮上忙，让企业得以相互分享风险情报的行业举措也能。

37
00:05:42,570 --> 00:05:50,030
This report will also argue that there is a need to provide incentives to improve cyber-security, be they carrots or sticks.
本报道还将论证，有必要提供激励措施来改善网络安全，无论是“胡萝卜”还是“大棒”。

38
00:05:50,830 --> 00:06:05,850
One idea is to encourage internet-service providers (ISPs), or the companies that manage internet connections, to shoulder more responsibility for identifying and helping to clean up computers infected with malicious software (malware).
一种思路是鼓励互联网服务提供商（ISP），即管理互联网连接的公司，在识别并帮助清理感染了恶意软件的电脑方面承担更多责任。

39
00:06:06,650 --> 00:06:15,610
Another is to find ways to ensure that software developers produce code with fewer flaws in it so that hackers have fewer security holes to exploit.
另一种思路是设法确保软件开发人员编写出缺陷更少的代码，从而让黑客可利用的安全漏洞更少。

40
00:06:16,410 --> 00:06:25,370
An additional reason for getting tech companies to give a higher priority to security is that cyberspace is about to undergo another massive change.
要让科技公司更优先考虑安全，还有一个理由：网络空间即将经历又一次巨变。

41
00:06:26,170 --> 00:06:37,830
Over the next few years billions of new devices, from cars to household appliances and medical equipment, will be fitted with tiny computers that connect them to the web and make them more useful.
未来几年，从汽车到家用电器再到医疗设备，数十亿台新设备将装上微型计算机，借此连上网络，变得更加有用。

42
00:06:38,630 --> 00:06:49,630
Dubbed “the internet of things”, this is already making it possible, for example, to control home appliances using smartphone apps and to monitor medical devices remotely.
这被称为“物联网”，已经让一些事情成为可能，例如用智能手机应用程序控制家用电器、远程监控医疗设备。

43
00:06:50,430 --> 00:06:58,430
But unless these systems have adequate security protection, the internet of things could easily become the internet of new things to be hacked.
但是，除非这些系统具备足够的安全防护，否则物联网很容易就会变成由一个个可供黑客入侵的新物件连成的网络。

44
00:06:59,230 --> 00:07:03,430
Plenty of people are eager to take advantage of any weaknesses they may spot.
很多人都急于利用自己可能发现的任何弱点。

45
00:07:04,230 --> 00:07:09,590
Hacking used to be about geeky college kids tapping away in their bedrooms to annoy their elders.
过去，黑客行为就是一些书呆子气的大学生在自己的卧室里不停地敲键盘，好让长辈们恼火。

46
00:07:10,390 --> 00:07:12,090
It has grown up with a vengeance.
如今，它已经长大，而且来势汹汹。
